    {
      "jobId": "89cede8e-7694-44cd-9035-84aae63e6ba1",
      "question": "Which assets had the strongest and weakest adjusted returns over the 20 trading sessions ending August 31, 2026, and how did their daily volatility compare?",
      "submittedAt": "2026-09-29T20:17:57.919558Z",
      "completedAt": "2026-09-29T20:19:49.679970Z",
      "status": "success",
      "report": {
        "markdown": "**Adjusted‑return leaders (20‑trading‑session window ending 2026‑08‑31)**\n\n| Rank | Asset (ID) | Adjusted return | Daily volatility (σ) |\n|------|------------|----------------|----------------------|\n| **1 (strongest)** | **asset‑aether** | **+0.211 %** (0.00211) | **1.59 %** (0.01589) |\n| 12 (weakest) | **asset‑cascade** | **‑15.25 %** (‑0.15249) | **1.16 %** (0.01156) |\n\n*All 12 reviewed assets were observed over 21 sessions (coverage = 100 %).*\n\n**Volatility comparison**\n- The top‑performing asset (**asset‑aether**) exhibited the **higher daily volatility** (≈ 1.59 %) among the two extremes.\n- The poorest performer (**asset‑cascade**) was **less volatile** (≈ 1.16 %).\n\nThus, the strongest return came from a relatively more volatile asset, while the weakest return came from a comparatively calmer one.\n\n**Limitations** – The scan covers the synthetic “reviewed_assets” universe; results are descriptive historical observations and not investment advice.\n\n[1]\n\n## Sources\n\n- [1] Market analytics result — market scan — evidence `hermes-receipt:db859d4ea67fbaceaa7e626be83b81524f1ac1aa1ef0f446c1314562a9672679`",
